WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.400
I really don't understand why
teachers put so much emphasis

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.160
on testing our comprehension.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.500
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today I
wanna talk about comprehension questions,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.480
how they relate to language learning.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.170
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.560
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.780
Follow me on a podcast service and
if you do, please leave a review.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.770
So comprehension questions are kind
of a staple for the language teaching

00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:36.220
community, uh, they wanna ask you, the
teachers, did you understand who, what

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.650
did this person do to that person?

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:44.710
And I'm, I'm reminded of this sort of, and
I must say, I've talked about this before.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.210
I I'm very sort of allergic
to comprehension questions.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.019
I don't like them.

00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:54.660
I think they're, they destroy the pleasure
of, uh, of reading or of listening.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:01:00.535
uh, and I, the reason I raised this is
because I decided to sort of investigate

00:01:01.135 --> 00:01:04.885
this, uh, language learning system
that the Canadian government has

00:01:04.915 --> 00:01:09.665
spent money on, uh, working together
with the, uh, Canadian Broadcasting

00:01:09.685 --> 00:01:11.395
Corporation and Radio Canada.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.460
Uh, to use sort of, uh, radio program
or television programs as a means

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.530
of helping Canadians learn the other
official language, or some people

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.729
maybe need to learn them both.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:24.210
I don't know.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.410
Uh, but a key element in this
program is comprehension questions.

00:01:29.429 --> 00:01:33.899
So I signed up, and, uh, they sort
of ask you what your level is.

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:36.539
And I said, this is for
French, I said, I'm advanced.

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:41.310
And then they, uh, give you a four
minute TV program and ask you questions

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.650
on it to sort of confirm that you
are the level that you said you

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.270
were, which I think is kind of silly.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:52.860
If I say I'm advanced, it's good enough
if I'm not, uh, it doesn't really matter.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.390
Uh, however, so comprehension
question is not really about

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:02.710
language, it's about trying to
remember what was in that program.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:06.220
And when you listen to a four
minute television program, you're

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.850
not concentrating the whole time
so it's very possible that you will

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.920
miss whatever detail it is that they
choose to ask you a question on.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.450
So it's actually kind of meaningless.

00:02:17.265 --> 00:02:21.195
And it also destroys the fun
of listening and reading.

00:02:21.195 --> 00:02:27.135
So I wanna quote to you, um, a, a
Brazilian educator, and when I was

00:02:27.135 --> 00:02:31.755
learning Brazilian or Portuguese,
I listened to a lot of his content.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.875
I bought, uh, CDs and he has a wonderful
voice and he has a wonderful, uh, attitude

00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:44.765
towards learning and, uh, quote to you
in English, what he said about questions.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:51.549
He said "There is a total incompatibility
between the joy of reading, a vagabond

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:56.760
experience, and the experience of
reading in order to answer questions

00:02:56.765 --> 00:02:58.769
and explain what you understood."

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.130
Now, this applies both
the reading and listening.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.480
We read and listen for pleasure.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.060
We read and listen in order to
allow our brains to get used

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.010
to the language we're learning.

00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:16.480
Uh, the more we listen and read the
better we learn, but of course we're

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.190
not concentrating the whole time.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.850
Uh, there are parts that we missed
because we weren't concentrating.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.870
There are parts that we don't simply
understand, which is also okay.

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:28.390
We understand 50, 60, 70 percent.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:34.530
I'm never interested in answering
questions on what it is I understood.

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:42.420
Now again, in this, uh, it's called Mauril
M A U R I L is the name of this app.

00:03:42.425 --> 00:03:44.970
You can find it in the, in the App Store.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.140
Uh, and the whole thing is built
around comprehension questions.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:55.770
So you can select a television program
to listen to that's either two or five

00:03:55.770 --> 00:04:00.765
or 10 minutes long and then you answer
questions, and if you answer the questions

00:04:00.765 --> 00:04:04.725
correctly, then you gradually can move
to the next stage, and the next stage.

00:04:04.785 --> 00:04:08.895
So answering comprehension questions
is also means of controlling you.

00:04:09.045 --> 00:04:13.785
So you can't just wander around
and, and, you know, study whatever

00:04:13.785 --> 00:04:17.714
text you wanna study or whatever TV
program you wanna watch and listen to

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.495
and read, you have to kind of follow
their progression and they control you

00:04:21.584 --> 00:04:23.265
with these comprehension questions.

00:04:23.685 --> 00:04:29.895
And very often, you know, two or
three potential answers are correct.

00:04:30.195 --> 00:04:34.125
Or maybe none of them really strikes
you as being correct, but you have

00:04:34.125 --> 00:04:38.955
to fit into the answer that they're
looking for, which also bothers me.

00:04:39.555 --> 00:04:45.075
Uh, and of course, teachers have to
prepare these comprehension questions.

00:04:45.075 --> 00:04:49.335
So it's not just a matter, like I
can go on YouTube and find content,

00:04:49.725 --> 00:04:56.315
uh, you know, whatever I want, uh, TV
program or a movie or, uh, documentary.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.660
And if there are, um, subtitles, I
can import it into LingQ and study it.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.130
Whatever I'm interested in, nobody has to
prepare comprehension questions for me.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.510
We don't need an army of, of teachers,
you know, preparing all the stuff

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.030
everything is available to learn from.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:17.640
And sometimes I understand it better
sometimes I understand it not so well.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:24.465
I listen to um, Persian, uh, podcasts,
uh, read through it gradually

00:05:24.465 --> 00:05:25.664
understand a little bit more.

00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:30.074
And it's part of this vagabond
experience as Rubem Alvez

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.325
says of enjoying the language.

00:05:32.745 --> 00:05:38.025
And if I had to answer comprehension
questions, um, I wouldn't, it

00:05:38.025 --> 00:05:39.435
simply destroys the fun of it.

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:42.915
So I, and, and, you know, it was funny.

00:05:42.915 --> 00:05:45.735
I, I remember when I was learning
Russian, there was a wonderful book,

00:05:46.275 --> 00:05:53.265
uh, on Russian history and they also
had audio with it but the audio didn't

00:05:53.265 --> 00:05:57.360
correspond to the text because somehow
it was felt that it was better.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.020
That reading is one activity and
listening is another activity

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.400
and the two don't connect.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.960
But to me, if I'm listening to something,
I then wanna be able to read it.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.890
And if I read it and look at words, I will
eventually understand more and more of it.

00:06:12.235 --> 00:06:15.055
Without having to answer
comprehension questions.

00:06:15.505 --> 00:06:19.225
Uh, so I, you know, I raised this
because I I've, it's a kind of

00:06:19.225 --> 00:06:20.305
a theme that I've worked before.

00:06:20.425 --> 00:06:25.735
I really don't understand why
teachers put so much emphasis

00:06:26.215 --> 00:06:28.525
on testing our comprehension.

00:06:28.945 --> 00:06:35.810
Now I do understand that, uh, in school
curricula, uh, schools are, are obliged

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.440
to test the students in order to
prove to the parents and to the school

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.980
administrators that the kids learn
something, which is very unfortunate,

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:49.310
because as I've said before, uh, in
Canada, the majority of kids who are in

00:06:49.315 --> 00:06:53.480
the regular French program after eight,
10 years, they can't speak French.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:58.130
So it really doesn't matter how well
they did on comprehension questions.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.620
In fact, they can't use the language.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:06.555
if on the other hand, the emphasis were
more on making the language important or

00:07:06.555 --> 00:07:12.615
interesting or fun, maybe more of them
would fall in love with the language.

00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:16.845
More of them would learn the
language than is the case right now.

00:07:17.145 --> 00:07:19.275
So nothing really new here.

00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:22.965
It's just that I happen to be,
you know, uh, uh, I wanted to

00:07:22.965 --> 00:07:28.635
test out this, this, uh, program
called, uh, Mauril, M A U R I L.

00:07:28.665 --> 00:07:32.325
And I was immediately confronted with
all these comprehension questions.

00:07:32.325 --> 00:07:34.245
And so I thought I would do a video.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:40.650
Um, remember though, the most important
thing in language learning is enjoyment.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.760
And if comprehension questions increase
your enjoyment of language learning

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:50.160
and in your enjoyment of listening
to and reading in the language, good.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:51.600
For me, it doesn't.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.140
So again, thank you for listening.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.440
And, uh, I have a couple of
videos that are somewhat related.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.720
So if you wanna follow up, you
can go there and have a look.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:01.440
Thank you.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:02.520
Bye-bye for now.

